lization of novelty beyond mechanistic explanations. Time, for Bergson, is not a static measure but a living process that continually reorganizes being and experience; the Whole of reality is a virtual totality that unfolds through moments of becoming, resisting simple spatialization.\n\nBergson’s theory of memory emphasizes its central role in perception and consciousness. In Matter and Memory, memory is not passive habit alone but consists of active and passive syntheses that shape how the present is reproduced and how the past informs present awareness. Memory comprises pure recollection and recollection-images, and processes such as translation, contraction, and expansion participate in how experiences are remembered and re interpreted. The past coexists with the present, continually informing perception and action, while consciousness itself is coextensive with life, continually drawing on memory to issue new expressions of reality.\n\nThe text situates Bergson as a proponent of intuition as a method for knowing and solving problems. Intuition, for Bergson, enables access to the deep structures of duration and life that elude formal analysis, and he distinguishes true problems from false ones through problem formulation and solution. This methodological stance underpins his broader critique of purely mechanical or dualistic schemes, advocating instead a monistic, dynamic view in which mind and matter are interwoven and continuously in tension and unity.\n\nElan vital, duration, and memory together form a framework for understanding how life transcends deterministic nature. Through creative evolution, life differentiates and actualizes virtual possibilities, generating new planes of nature and new instruments of freedom. In this Open Totality, the past is essential to present experience, and intelligence arises from the ongoing synthesis of duration, memory, and perception.\n\nThe text references Bergson’s influential works—Creative Evolution (CE), Matter and Me